+
+[[configurable-collection-implementations]]
+==== Configuring collection implementation types
+
+The implementation used for any supported interface in the table above can be replaced in a Java properties resource.
+By default, MapStruct looks for `mapstruct.properties` at the root of the mapping compilation classpath. The
+`mapstruct.configurationFile` annotation processor option selects another resource by relative path on that same
+classpath. An explicitly selected resource must exist; when the default resource is absent, the built-in implementation
+types remain in effect.
+
+Configured entries use the fully-qualified interface and implementation names:
+
+[source, properties]
+----
+mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.List=com.example.FastList
+mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.Map=com.example.OrderedMap
+----
+
+The implementation can be declared in the current compilation, including by another annotation processor in a later
+processing round. It must be a publicly accessible, concrete class that:
+
+* is assignable to the configured interface;
+* declares the same number of unbounded type parameters and preserves them in the same order;
+* has a public no-argument constructor that declares no checked exceptions.
+
+If the class also has a public `int` constructor that declares no checked exceptions, MapStruct passes the source size
+as its initial capacity whenever the size is available. Otherwise, the no-argument constructor is used. An applicable
+object factory takes precedence over the configured implementation.
+
+Unknown properties, unsupported interfaces, empty values, conflicting duplicate entries, and invalid implementation
+types cause compilation to fail. Repeated entries with the same value are allowed.
